{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts"]]},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/LexicographicProductView"},"metadata":{"title":"LexicographicProductView","symbolKind":"struct","externalID":"s:13GraphProducts24LexicographicProductViewV","modules":[{"name":"GraphProducts"}],"roleHeading":"Structure","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"LexicographicProductView"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"LexicographicProductView","kind":"identifier"}],"remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/GraphProducts\/LexicographicProductView.swift#L39","fileName":"LexicographicProductView.swift"}},"sections":[],"abstract":[{"text":"The lexicographic product G ∘ H of two graphs, both directed or both undirected, as a lazy","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"view. Made by ","type":"text"},{"code":"lexicographicProductView(_:_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Also called the composition G[H].","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"LexicographicProductView"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"First"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"Second"},{"kind":"text","text":">"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"overview","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Vertices and their indices are as in ","type":"text"},{"code":"CartesianProductView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". The row of (g, h) lists ","type":"text"},{"code":"(g′, h′)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"eac